Strings 

If you try to assign the blue value to $color without the quotes, Powershell will return an error. 

$color = blue

Without quotes, PowerShell, interprets blue as a command and tries to execute it. Because the command blue doesn't exist, 
PowerShell returns an error message that says so. To correctly define a string, you need to use quotes around your value. 

Combining Strings and Variables

Strings aren't restricted to word; they can be phrases and sentences as well. 
For instance, you can assign $sentence this string:

$sentence = "Today, you learned that PowerShell loves the color blue" 

But maybe you want to use this same sentence, but with the words Powershell and blue as the values of variables. 
For instance, what if oyu have a variable called $name, another called $language and other called $color?

$language = 'PowerShell'
$color = 'blue'

$sentence = "Today, you learned that $language loves the color $color"
$sentence

Notice, the use of double quotes. Enclosing your sentence in single quotes doesn't achieve the intended result. 
There's an important difference between single and double quotes in Powershell. 

Using Double Vs. Single Quotes

When assigning a variable a simple string, you can use single or double quotes.

Page 22

$color = "yellow"
$color = 'red'
$color = ' '

It doesn't matter which quotes you use to define a simple string. 
So why does it matter when you have variables in your string?
The answer has to do with variable interpolation, or variable expansion.

Normally, when you enter $color by itself into the console and hit ENTER, PowerShell interpolates, or expands
that variable. 

These are fancy terms that mean PowerShell is reading the value inside a variable, or opening the box so you can see inside. 

When you use double quotes to call a variable, the same thing happen: the variable is expanded. 

"$color" <- This will read inside

'$color' <- This does not read inside. 

When you use single quotes, the console outputs the variable itself. 

Page 23

Single quotes tell PowerShell that you mean exactly what you're typing, whether that's a word like blue or what looks like a variable 
called $color. 

So when you use a variable inside single quotes, PowerShell doesn't know how to expand that variable's value. 
This is why you need to use double quotes when inserting variables into your strings. 

Objects

In PowerShell, everything is an object. 

In technical terms, an object is an individual instance of a specific template, called a class. 

A class specifies the kinds of things an object will contain. 

An object's class determines its methods, or actions that can be taken on that object. 

In other words, the methods are all the things an object can do. 

  For example, a list object might have a sort() method, when called, will sort the list. 
  Likewise, an object's class determines its properties, the object's variables. 
  You can think of the properties as all the data about the object. 

