Data Structures 

  A data structure is a way to organize multiple pieces of data. Data Structures in PowerShell are represented by objects stored in variables and come in three main types: arrays, ArrayLists, and hashtables. 

  Arrays

    If a simple variable (such as a float type) is a single box, then an array is a whole bunch of boxes taped together- a list of items represented by a single variable. 

  Defining Arrays

    First, let's define a variable called $colorPicker and assign it an array that holds four colors as strings. 
    To do this, you use the sign(@) followed by the four strings(separated by commas) within parentheses. 

    $colorPicker = @('blue', 'white', 'yellow', 'black')

    The @ sign followed by an opening parenthesis and zero or more elements separated by a comma signals to PowerShell that you'd like to create an array.

  Reading Array Elements

    To access an element in an array, you use the name of the array followed by a pair of square brackets ([]) that contain the index of the element you want to access. 
    As with string characters, you start numbering arrays at 0, so the first element is at index 0, the second at index 1, and so on. 
    In Powershell, using -1 as the index will return the final element. 

    If you try to specify an index number that doesn't exist in the array, Powershell will return an error message. 
    To access multiple elements in an array at the same timem you can use the range operator between two numbers. 
    The range operator will make Powershell return those two numbers and every number between them. 

    PS> 1..3

    To use the range operator to access multiple items in an array, you use a range for an index. 

    PS>$colorPicker[1..3]

[Page 28]

Modifying Elements in an Array 

  If you want to change an element in an array, you can reference an item with its index and use  the equal sign to assign a new value. 

  $colorPicker[3] = 'white'

Adding Elements to an Array

  You can add items to an array with the the addition operator(+). 

  $colorPicker = $colorPicker + 'orange'

  The reason for adding colorPicker to itself is because Powershell is being asked to interpolate the $colorPicker variable and then add a new element. 

  $colorPicker += 'brown'

  The += operator tekks PowerShell to add this item to the existing array. 

  You can also add arrays to other arrays. 

  $colorPicker += @('pink', 'cyan')

  Note that Powershell treats any comma separated set of values as an array and you don't explicitly need the parentheses. 

  Unfortunately, there is no equivalent of += to remove an element from an array. 

ArrayLists

  Everytime you add an element to an array, you're actually creating a new array from your old interpolated array and the new element. 
  The same thing happens when you remove an element from an array: PowerShell destroys your old array and makes a new one. 
  This is because arrays in PowerShell have a fixed size.

  For small arrays, you won't notice the difference, but when you begin to work with huge arrays, you'll see a performance hit. 
  If you know you'll have to remove or add many elements than you can use an ArrayList. 
  ArrayLists behave nearly identical to the typical PowerShell array, but with one crucial difference: they can dyanmically adjust to add or remoe elements. 

  Defining an ArrayList is exactly like defining an array, except that you need to cast it as an ArrayList. 

  $colorPicker = [System.Collections.ArrayList]@('blue', 'white', 'yellow', 'black')

  As with an array, when you call an ArrayList, each item is displayed on a separate line. (Page 30)

Adding Elements to an ArrayList

  To add or remove an element from an ArrayList without destroying it, you can use its methods. 
  You can use the Add() and Remove() methods to add or remove items from an ArrayList. 

  $colorPicker.Add('gray')

  This will give you the index of the new element. If you don't want to see the index of the new element than you do assignment to the null variable. 

  $null = $colorPicker.Add('gray')

Removing Elements from an ArrayList

  You can remove elements in a similar way, using the Remove() method. 

  $colorPicker.Remove('gray')

  Notice that to remove an item, you don't have to know the index number. You can reference the element by its actual value. If the array has multiple elements with 
  the same value, Powershell will remove the element closeest to the start of the ArrayList.
  The rule of thumb is the larger the collection of items you're working with, the better off you'll be using an ArrayList. 
  If you're working with small arrays of fewer than 100 elements ro so, you'll notice little differenece between an array and an ArrayList. 

Hashtables

  Arrays and ArrayLists are great when you need your data associated with only a position in a list. But sometimes you'll want something more direct: a way to correlate
  two pieces of data.
  In that case, you could use a hashtable(dictionary), a PowerShell data structure that contains a list of key-value pairs. (Page 31)
